# Mobile Architecture Overview

Architecture tells you **where code belongs, who owns state, and what happens when an operation fails**. Follow Clean Architecture boundaries and the documented network-resilience and circuit-breaker patterns across the supported mobile platforms.

**React Native is preferred for new applications.** Android and iOS remain supported; follow the assigned stack for an existing application. React Native uses **Redux Toolkit where Redux state is required**.

> **Confidential — internal use only.** Keep enterprise architecture, source, and internal contracts within approved systems. Use synthetic data in examples. Never include credentials, production data, or sensitive diagnostics in diagrams, code, or AI prompts.

## 1. Follow one feature: place a chicken order

We use **My Chicken App** throughout Architecture and Platform & SDKs. A user selects a quantity, submits an order, and sees either a confirmed order or an accurately described unresolved outcome.

This is a teaching example. It does not define an enterprise endpoint, request schema, SDK method, payment flow, or backend idempotency contract.

| Responsibility | Owns in the chicken-order feature | Keep out |
|---|---|---|
| **UI and state owner** | Render quantity and submission state, collect actions, prevent overlapping submissions, and reject stale account/session results. | Transport parsing, database queries, and privileged credentials. |
| **Domain / use case** | Validate the input and coordinate the order operation through a domain-facing contract. | Screens, Redux store shapes, concrete HTTP clients, and database types. |
| **Repository contract and implementation** | Describe the required order operation and implement the approved data policy. Map responses into domain outcomes. | Navigation and screen rendering. |
| **Data adapters** | Perform concrete network or permitted local-storage operations. | Deciding what screen to show or treating cached data as proof that an order was accepted. |
| **Application composition** | Construct implementations and inject them into the feature. | Hidden business rules inside startup wiring. |

The backend remains responsible for authorization and authoritative order processing. Client validation and disabled buttons do not provide server-side duplicate protection.

## 2. Understand the runtime flow

