| Part | Plain meaning |
|---|---|
| **Repository** | Coordinates data access and converts the API response into the feature's result. |
| **API client** | Handles the actual request and response through the established transport setup. |
| **Backend** | Authorizes and processes the order. It is not part of the mobile app's data layer. |

The diagram shows the confirmed-response path. If a response is lost after submission, the result can be **unknown**; do not show a confirmation or resend automatically. A confirmed response must still belong to the current account/request before updating the screen.

Local storage is deliberately omitted here. Add it only when the application's approved design requires it. The domain-facing repository contract is explained on the next page; it is a code boundary, not an extra request sent over the network.

## 5. Map the responsibilities to your platform

| Responsibility | Android | iOS | React Native |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Render the feature** | Compose or the established View UI. | SwiftUI or the established UIKit UI. | React Native components. |
| **Own UI-facing state** | Lifecycle-aware ViewModel. | Appropriately isolated observable model / ViewModel. | Local component state where sufficient; Redux Toolkit for Redux-owned feature state. |
| **Execute the use case** | Kotlin operation using domain-facing interfaces. | Swift operation using domain-facing protocols. | TypeScript operation using domain-facing contracts. |
| **Access concrete data** | Repository implementation and network/storage adapters. | Repository implementation and network/storage adapters. | Repository implementation and network/storage adapters. |
| **Connect dependencies** | Existing composition / DI mechanism. | Application composition and explicit dependency construction. | Application composition and the established injection mechanism. |

The next page develops the feature contracts and platform code. Platform & SDKs supplies the platform-specific implementation standards; this overview does not introduce a new DI library or private SDK API.

## 6. Define submission behavior before coding

| Situation | Required behavior |
|---|---|
| Invalid quantity | Show validation feedback without sending the order. Define actual allowed values with the feature owner. |
| Submission in progress | Expose submitting state and prevent overlapping submissions from this owner. |
| Confirmed response | Display the confirmed result only for the current account and request. Prevent accidental resubmission of that completed operation. |
| Timeout or cancellation after dispatch | Treat the business outcome as unknown unless the approved contract proves otherwise. Reconcile before another submission. |
| Circuit breaker rejects a call before dispatch | Report dependency unavailability according to the operation contract. A breaker does not resolve an earlier order with an unknown outcome. |
| Navigation, logout, or account switch | Respect lifetime ownership and block stale updates. Cancellation does not undo a server-side write. |
| App process ends | In-memory flags disappear. Durable recovery requires the application's approved persistence and reconciliation design. |
| Feature flag disables ordering | Apply the configured client behavior. Server authorization and business rules remain mandatory. |

Test these behaviors at their owning boundaries. **100% production-code coverage is required under the supported metrics defined in Testing & Quality**; coverage does not replace meaningful assertions or device verification.

