# Feature Architecture — Place a Chicken Order

**Build the feature in this order:** define its result → define the repository contract → implement data access → connect the use case → connect the screen's state owner.

The examples below implement the **order core**, an injected API port, and fake composition. They do not call a real service or provide a complete screen. React Native additionally includes Redux Toolkit coordination. Platform UI and native lifecycle implementations belong in Platform & SDKs.

> **Confidential — internal use only.** All identifiers and results below are synthetic. Keep enterprise APIs, credentials, source, and operational evidence in approved systems. Never place real orders to verify these examples.

## 1. Follow one request

<MermaidZoom title="Place a chicken order — request out, result back">

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant UI as Screen / State Owner
    participant USE as Place Chicken Order
    participant REPO as Repository
    participant API as API Client
    participant SERVER as Backend

    UI->>UI: Enter submitting state
    UI->>USE: Submit the selected quantity
    USE->>USE: Validate the input
    Note over USE: Invalid input returns without a repository call
    USE->>REPO: Submit valid input through the contract
    REPO->>API: Use the injected API adapter
    API->>SERVER: Send the approved request
    SERVER-->>API: Return the order response
    API-->>REPO: Return response data
    REPO->>REPO: Map into a domain result
    REPO-->>USE: Confirmed or unknown
    USE-->>UI: Return the result
    Note over UI: Update only the current account and request
```

</MermaidZoom>

**Repository:** coordinates data access and maps the result. **API client:** performs the actual transport. **Backend:** authorizes and processes the order; it is outside the mobile app. The diagram shows a response arriving. A lost response can leave the order outcome unknown.

## 2. Set the example contract

| Input / result | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `accountId` / `accountID` | Active account supplied by the session owner. This value is not authorization. |
| `requestId` / `requestID` | Identity for one intended order attempt. It is not automatically a backend idempotency key. |
| `itemId` / `itemID` | Synthetic selected item, such as `chicken-grilled`. |
| `quantity` | Teaching rule: integer from 1 through 20. Replace with confirmed product requirements. |
| `confirmed` | The example adapter received a nonblank order ID in its expected response representation. |
| `invalid` | Local validation failed; no repository call occurred. |
| `unknown` | No usable confirmation is available after the adapter was invoked. Reconcile before another submission. |

These types are **example-owned contracts**, not an enterprise HTTP schema. Production mapping must validate the approved backend response, account/request association, and failure semantics. A known server rejection needs an explicitly defined domain result; these examples conservatively classify unrecognized responses/errors as unknown.

## 3. Put each responsibility in the correct location

```text
src/
  ui/                 # Screen, state owner, Redux slice where applicable
  domain/             # OrderRequest, OrderResult, OrderRepository, use case
  repository/         # RemoteOrderRepository and response mapping
  data/               # OrderApi and the concrete approved HTTP adapter
```

Use the project's Kotlin packages, Swift targets, or TypeScript modules to express these responsibilities. Domain code must not import concrete transport, UI, or Redux implementations. Wire concrete dependencies at application composition.

The platform blocks are grouped into single files so the example core can be read and checked together. Their comments identify the declarations to separate when applying the boundaries in the real repository. A single example file does not enforce those module boundaries.

## 4. Copy and inspect your platform core

Use the existing project's compatible toolchain. No HTTP, database, or DI package is introduced by these examples.

