## 5. Connect the real API at one boundary

Replace the demo API implementation with the application's approved transport adapter. Keep the use case dependent on the repository contract.

| Confirm with the owner | Implement in the adapter / integration |
|---|---|
| Endpoint, authorization, request and response schemas | Use the established authenticated client and response validation. Do not infer an endpoint from this page. |
| Idempotency and reconciliation | Preserve the approved operation identity and reconcile unknown outcomes. |
| Timeout and error semantics | Classify known rejection versus unknown outcome using the actual service contract. |
| Retry ownership | Use one approved policy. No automatic business-write retries are present here. |
| Account changes and process recovery | Prevent stale writes/results and recover unresolved operations according to the approved persistence/session design. |

**In-memory guards do not provide durable duplicate protection.** Account reset, app restart, a second store/service instance, another device, and in-flight backend writes all require coordinated server/session handling. Ignoring a stale UI result does not cancel the business operation.

## 6. Verify the boundaries

| Exercise | Expected behavior |
|---|---|
| Valid synthetic request | One adapter call and the mapped confirmation. |
| Blank identifier or invalid quantity | `invalid`; no adapter call. |
| Missing/blank order ID or adapter error | `unknown`; no fabricated confirmation or automatic retry. |
| Native task cancellation | Cancellation propagates; dispatched-write uncertainty remains subject to recovery. |
| Repeated Redux submission while pending | One adapter call through the retained store. |
| Redux submission after confirmed/unknown | Blocked until an approved lifecycle transition exists. |
| Account changes before Redux response | Previous account's response does not update the new account's state. |

Use the repository's actual unit-test and coverage commands. Native UI/device tests, complete screens, and 100% production-code coverage enforcement are covered in Testing & Quality and the platform standards. These core examples alone do not establish application-wide coverage or production readiness.

