# Network Resilience & Circuit Breakers

**A request failure is not always an order failure.** If My Chicken App sends an order and loses the response, the backend may still have created it. Show an unknown outcome and reconcile before another submission.

Use this page after [Feature Architecture](./mobile-architecture.mdx). Put resilience at the approved network/data boundary, not in individual screen tap handlers.

> **Confidential — internal use only.** Use synthetic failures in tests. Keep private endpoints, credentials, raw response bodies, account details, and signing material out of logs, examples, and support messages.

## 1. Choose the right mechanism

| Mechanism | Plain meaning | Chicken-order consequence |
|---|---|---|
| **Timeout** | Stop waiting after the configured transport/operation budget. | The server may already have accepted the order. |
| **Cancellation** | Stop obsolete client work where supported. | Leaving the screen does not cancel a server-side order. |
| **Retry** | Make another attempt. | Do not repeat submission without approved replay/idempotency semantics. |
| **Circuit breaker** | Stop calling a dependency temporarily after qualifying failures. | New calls can be rejected before reaching the network. Earlier orders can still be unresolved. |
| **Reconciliation** | Read the authoritative status through an approved contract. | Recover the original outcome instead of blindly submitting again. |

A circuit breaker does not supply offline ordering, durable duplicate protection, or a guarantee against application crashes.

## 2. Understand the three circuit states

