## 6. Show the correct outcome to the user

| Event | What happens next |
|---|---|
| Local validation fails | Correct the input. No network call; no outage count. |
| Circuit rejects before the current attempt starts | Show temporary unavailability under the operation contract. Do not spin in an immediate retry loop. |
| Approved submission returns confirmation | Show the current account/request's confirmed result. |
| Submission times out or response is unusable | Mark the order unknown and start approved recovery. No fabricated confirmation. |
| User cancels after dispatch | Stop client work where supported; preserve uncertainty about the backend result. |
| Authentication expires or access is denied | Follow the approved session/authorization flow. Do not treat this as dependency availability failure by default. |
| Service rate-limits a request | Follow its explicit delay and replay contract. Do not assume every rate limit is a server outage. |
| Account switches | Reject stale UI updates and coordinate in-flight writes/recovery with the session owner. |

The preceding feature-core example conservatively maps adapter exceptions to `unknown` because its private failure contract is unspecified. If the real adapter can **prove no dispatch occurred**, introduce an explicit unavailable/not-sent domain outcome and update the use case, UI, and tests together. Do not relabel all errors as safely retryable.

## 7. Reconcile an unknown chicken order

